<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sanuki Province</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sanuki Province was an old province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shikoku">Shikoku</a>, corresponding to modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagawa-prefecture">Kagawa Prefecture</a>. It was the birthplace of the monk Kūkai and home of the Konpira shrine. Briefly conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chosokabe-motochika">Chōsokabe Motochika</a> before Hideyoshi's invasion of 1585, it was given to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikoma-chikamasa">Ikoma Chikamasa</a>, who built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takamatsu">Takamatsu</a> castle from 1588; under the Tokugawa its main domain was Takamatsu.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sanuki-province.jpg" alt="Sanuki Province" width="320" height="335" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ash_Crow · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Provinces_of_Japan-Sanuki.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
